	(text_box "THE STORAGE DECISION - A BOUNDED APPEND-ONLY REGION ON RAW SECTORS. NO FILESYSTEM.\n\nDECIDED: a microSD card, written as RAW 512-BYTE SECTORS in SPI mode, as one append-only region of\nfixed-stride records starting at a fixed sector. No FAT, no directory, no file. Read at the desk with\ndd, not by a firmware USB stack.\n\nWHAT THE WRITE PATH BECOMES.  sector = REGION_BASE + (sequence * STRIDE) / 512.  That is the whole\naddress calculation. No allocator, no directory entry, no cluster chain, no free-space search, and\ntherefore no unbounded worst case anywhere in it. #51 already fixes a bounded maximum record size at\ncomptime, which is exactly the primitive this needs.\n\nWHAT FAT32 WOULD HAVE COST, stated so the rover story does not rediscover it. A FAT32 writer needs\ndirectory-entry management, long-file-name handling, a cluster allocator, and a free-cluster search\nwhose worst case depends on the state of the card rather than on anything this firmware controls. It\nwould be a larger subproject than the rover firmware it exists to serve. And it makes every power cut\nstrictly worse: appending to a file touches the data cluster, the FAT chain and the directory entry -\nthree writes at three distant addresses - so an interrupted append can lose the FILESYSTEM rather than\none record. Append-only at a fixed stride puts none of OUR metadata in the path of a power cut.\n\n  READ THAT LAST SENTENCE PRECISELY. It says our metadata. It does NOT say the card is safe - see the\n  power sheet, where the SD specification's own wording is quoted. Removing the filesystem removes the\n  damage this design is responsible for; the card's internal flash translation layer remains exposed\n  and no host-side choice can fix that.\n\nWHAT THE RAW-SECTOR CHOICE COSTS, honestly.\n  * The card is not mountable. Plugging it into a laptop offers to format it - a real hazard, and the\n    reason the card is labelled and the region begins with a magic header the desk tooling checks.\n  * Capacity is bounded by the region, not by the card. An 8 GB card is ~15.6 M sectors; at a 64 B\n    record and 5 Hz that is more than a year of continuous rover logging, and at ~2 kB/s of raw\n    observations it is still ~1000 hours of base logging. Capacity is not the binding constraint.\n  * Nothing enumerates the log for you. Sequence numbers do that instead, which #51 already requires.\n\nWHY NOT AN SPI NOR FLASH PART, which would have given a genuinely bounded page-program time and no\nflash translation layer to corrupt. The base logs raw observations continuously; a full multi-band\nRXM-RAWX epoch runs to roughly 2 kB, so a 16 MB part holds about two hours and a 64 MB part about\nnine. A base station whose log wraps in an afternoon is not a base station. That is the trade this\nproject makes: capacity and a removable medium, against a storage device whose failure modes under\npower loss are not fully specified by anyone."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 15.24 261.6792 0)
		(size 122.1768 81.1812)
